    "summary": "Donald MacKay reconceptualized information as an action, not a thing — a verb-like process enacted within context. This formulation implies embodiment, contrasting with [[Claude-Shannon]]'s statistical definition that excluded meaning for mathematical precision. MacKay argued that information arises when an agent performs an act of distinction; meaning was regained at the cost of formal tractability.",
