    "summary": "Jane Bennett — political theorist and philosopher. She articulated [[vibrant-matter]]: matter possesses its own vitality, agency, and thing-power, challenging the passive inert matter assumed by modern philosophy. Her framework provides the political-ontological ground for treating matter as active participant in sonic composition.",
    "examples": "",
    "relations": "[[vibrant-matter]] + [[sonic-flux|Cox's sonic flux]]: Together they extend [[spectralism|spectralism's]] vibrational ontology into posthumanist frameworks. Spectralism's FFT and microtonality, read through Bennett, treat sound as an active participant in sonic composition — a phenomenological intra-active event where matter asserts its own sonic agency [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n\n[[vibrational-ontology]]: Bennett's vibrant matter extends vibrational ontology into political ecology — material agency as active, not passive [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n[[sonic-flux]]: Bennett's vibrant matter aligns with sonic flux — sound as real material force, a vibrational continuum preceding human perception [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n[[spectralism]]: Spectralism's FFT-based composition, read through Bennett, treats sound as an active participant in sonic composition — a phenomenological intra-active event [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n\n\n- See also: [[new-materialism]]",
