    "summary": "Rosa Menkman, glitch artist and theorist, authored the *Glitch Studies Manifesto*. She warns glitch aesthetics undergo rapid commodification — every glitch becomes fashion — eroding its subversive force. Her analysis positions the scar as permanent material trace against glitch as consumable aesthetic.",
    "examples": "",
    "relations": "[[Jussi-Parikka]] + [[media-archaeology]]: Parikka's media archaeology contextualizes Menkman's glitch art within the broader history of media materiality and the erasure paradigm, where glitches reveal the apparatus's material conditions [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n[[glitch]] + [[scar]]: Menkman's analysis of glitch co-optation cautions that the proposal's glitch-as-scar requires errors produce irremovable material transformations, resisting recuperation into aesthetic novelty [[Distilled_Tracing_the_Scar__Material_Agency_from_Feedback_Loops_to_Sed_v1_d4]].\n\n[[glitch-theory]] + [[erasure-paradigm]]: Glitch art's capacity to reveal the apparatus is undercut by its rapid commodification and dependence on an already-critical audience [[Distilled_Tracing_the_Scar__Material_Agency_from_Feedback_Loops_to_Sed_v1_d4]].",
