  "emergence": {
    "id": "emergence",
    "title": "emergence",
    "type": "term",
    "cluster": "A",
    "tags": [
      "- morphogenesis"
    ],
    "summary": "The process by which reorganization creates more complex structure with higher energy flow, making the system more susceptible to fluctuations and subsequent collapse or reorganization. An organism must have a responsive network of subsystems capable of autonomous behavior for emergence to occur. Irregularities of the environment function as opportunities around which the organism shapes its identity. In complex systems, emergence generates diversity, not uniformity; behavior is non-linear and context-specific. Material forms of living things are a diagram of the forces that have acted on them — form emerges from process [[Distilled_cybernetics-21-notes]].",
    "examples": "",
    "relations": "- [[self-organization]]: emergence is the observable result of self-organization — local interactions producing global patterns [[Distilled_cybernetics-21-notes]].\r\n\r\n\r\n- See also: [[complex-network-theory]]\r\n- See also: [[morphogenesis]]\r\n\r\n### Contribution from [[Distilled_beginning of heaven_Notes]]\r\n\r\nEmergence is the ability of an observer to perceive new organization or properties within a system. It occurs when a non-trivial system operates recursively, producing unexpected levels of order.",
    "keyExtracts": "",
    "crossLinks": [
      "self-organization",
      "complex-network-theory",
      "morphogenesis"
    ]
  },
