    "title": "open assemblage",
    "type": "term",
    "cluster": "B",
    "tags": [
      "deleuze",
      "assemblage",
      "connectivity"
    ],
    "summary": "[[Gilles-Deleuze]]'s concept (with [[Felix-Guattari]]) of a structure that is open, connective, and non-totalizing — remaining in contact with its outside and continually transforming through encounters. Betin draws on this to theorize the feedback loop as an open structure that accumulates irreversible scars through iterative material encounters rather than cycling toward equilibrium.",
    "examples": "",
    "relations": "- [[feedback-loop]]: Betin draws on open assemblage to theorize the loop as having no reset — always already scarred by prior iterations [[Betin2026-Tracing-the-Scar]].\n- [[non-trivial-machine]]: the fold or NTM may bridge the gap between assemblage's reconfigurability and irreversible scarring [[Distilled_Tracing_the_Scar__Material_Agency_from_Feedback_Loops_to_Sed_v1_d4]].\n\n- See also: [[Manuel-Delanda]]",
    "keyExtracts": "- \"A structure that accumulates its history as constitutive marks\" [[Betin2026-Tracing-the-Scar]].",
