    "title": "threshold drift",
    "type": "term",
    "cluster": "B",
    "tags": [
      "sensor",
      "meta-cut",
      "functional"
    ],
    "summary": "The sensor's functional meta-cut. When a microcontroller logs its own corrosion-induced sensor drift and automatically calibrates its polling frequency, it performs a functional meta-cut. The sensor becomes a [[non-trivial-machine]]: its output is no longer a direct reflection of input but continuously modified by its own sedimented internal history. Threshold drift is a functional, non-normative response to [[material-impedance]] — the machine accommodating its own decay without symbolic mediation. It is the technological instance of the [[distributed-meta-cut]], proving that self-modification in response to impedance is not exclusive to biological or ethical systems.",
    "examples": "",
    "relations": "- [[threshold-drift]] + [[non-trivial-machine]]: The sensor becomes a non-trivial machine — output modified by sedimented internal history [[Distilled_Conversation 1]].\n- [[threshold-drift]] + [[distributed-meta-cut]]: Threshold drift is the technological instance of distributed meta-cut — self-modification in response to impedance [[Distilled_Conversation 1]].\n- [[threshold-drift]] + [[material-impedance]]: The sensor accommodates its own decay as a functional response to material impedance [[Distilled_Conversation 1]].\n\n\n- See also: [[Heidi-Tikka]]\n- See also: [[invisible-pleats-installation]]\n- See also: [[meta-cut-distribution]]\n- See also: [[surrender-of-mastery]]",
