    "type": "term",
    "cluster": "A",
    "tags": [
      "live-coding",
      "pattern-theory",
      "algorithmic-composition",
      "haskell"
    ],
    "summary": "A live coding environment for pattern manipulation written in Haskell, using a mini-notation for defining rhythmic and melodic patterns manipulated in real time. Euclidean rhythm syntax and polyrhythmic layering techniques (including live manipulation of `n`, `k`, rotation parameters) are a critical gap in documented practice — the formal vocabulary exists but lacks practitioner case studies or tutorials.",
    "examples": "",
    "relations": "- [[tidalcycles]] + [[supercollider]]: Compared for sonic-political implications — educational accessibility, open-source ideologies, decolonial potential [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n- [[tidalcycles]] + [[strudel]]: Strudel ports TidalCycles' pattern paradigm to the browser — different access models, same pattern theory [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n- [[tidalcycles]] + [[euclidean-rhythms]]: Euclidean rhythm syntax in TidalCycles is underdocumented — live polyrhythmic entanglement lacks practitioner case studies [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n- [[tidalcycles]] + [[algorave]]: TidalCycles is a primary platform for algorave — pattern manipulation in performance context [[Distilled_Lets_make_a_research_for_the_audio_part_of_the_artwork__The__v0_d2]].\n\n\n- See also: [[bjorklund-algorithm]]\n- See also: [[Godfried-Toussaint]]",
